INSIGHT: the walk is mostly INSIDE changes (diagonal flips)
with occasional OUTSIDE changes (boundary flips).
The inside changes accumulate until the outside 'tips over'
into the lonely state.

This is the HIDDEN DEPENDENCE: the inside drives the outside,
but not directly — through the cycle structure and the
tournament's metagraph position.

======================================================================
PART 3: Dihedral symmetry and LRC
======================================================================

n=5: D_5 = rotations × reflections
  regular tournament: vertex-transitive, self-complementary
  D_5 orbit of arcs = 2 diagonal classes (d=1,...,2)
  observer at vertex 0: breaks D_5 to Z_2 (reflection only)
  surviving symmetry: t ↔ 1-t (THM-387 time reversal)

  diagonal coupling to boundary:
    d=1: coupling ~ 1/1 = 1.000
    d=2: coupling ~ 1/2 = 0.500

n=7: D_7 = rotations × reflections
  regular tournament: vertex-transitive, self-complementary
  D_7 orbit of arcs = 3 diagonal classes (d=1,...,3)
  observer at vertex 0: breaks D_7 to Z_2 (reflection only)
  surviving symmetry: t ↔ 1-t (THM-387 time reversal)

  diagonal coupling to boundary:
    d=1: coupling ~ 1/1 = 1.000
    d=2: coupling ~ 1/2 = 0.500
    d=3: coupling ~ 1/3 = 0.333

n=14: D_14 = rotations × reflections
  n is even: no regular tournament (all scores must be (n-1)/2)
  nearest: near-regular tournament (scores differ by 1)
  D_14 orbit: 7 diagonal classes
  observer at vertex 0: breaks D_14 to Z_2 (reflection only)
  surviving symmetry: t ↔ 1-t (THM-387 time reversal)

  diagonal coupling to boundary:
    d=1: coupling ~ 1/1 = 1.000
    d=2: coupling ~ 1/2 = 0.500
    d=3: coupling ~ 1/3 = 0.333
    d=4: coupling ~ 1/4 = 0.250
    d=5: coupling ~ 1/5 = 0.200
    d=6: coupling ~ 1/6 = 0.167
    d=7: coupling ~ 1/7 = 0.143

======================================================================
PART 4: Inside/outside cascade — why n=7 is the threshold
======================================================================

n=5: inside/outside = 5/5 = 1.0
  cascade value: 0.8640 < 1 ✗
  observer richness: 2 diagonals / 2 boundary = 1.0

n=6: inside/outside = 9/6 = 1.5
  cascade value: 0.9877 < 1 ✗
  observer richness: 3 diagonals / 2 boundary = 1.5

n=7: inside/outside = 14/7 = 2.0
  cascade value: 1.1156 ≥ 1 ✓
  observer richness: 4 diagonals / 2 boundary = 2.0

n=8: inside/outside = 20/8 = 2.5
  cascade value: 1.2458 ≥ 1 ✓
  observer richness: 5 diagonals / 2 boundary = 2.5

n=14: inside/outside = 77/14 = 5.5
  cascade value: 2.0445 ≥ 1 ✓
  observer richness: 11 diagonals / 2 boundary = 5.5

THE THRESHOLD EXPLAINED:
  The cascade works when the inside is rich enough to control the outside.
  Observer richness (n-3)/2 measures this:
    n=5: richness 1.0 — barely enough inside → cascade fails
    n=6: richness 1.5 — not quite → cascade barely fails (0.988)
    n=7: richness 2.0 — just enough → cascade works (1.116)
    n=14: richness 5.5 — abundant → cascade easy (1.766)

  The regular polygon's INSIDE (diagonals/tiles/cycles) drives
  the OUTSIDE (boundary/gaps/scores). When inside >> outside,
  the hidden structure has enough freedom to force loneliness.

